  "articleBody": "Hiring a contractor to tile your basement requires looking past basic surface aesthetics. Basements present unique subterranean moisture and structural movement challenges that upstairs living spaces never face, making proper vetting essential before signing any work agreement.\n\nWhat to Ask Before You Decide\n- Moisture evaluation: Ask specifically how they test the slab. Standard installations require moisture levels around 75% relative humidity or lower using tests like ASTM F2170, and a qualified installer must verify this before laying materials.\n- Crack management: Ask how they address concrete imperfections. A quality installer will not simply mortar over slab movement; they should propose repairing cracks or applying an uncoupling crack-isolation membrane.\n- Material acclimation: Ensure the contractor plans to acclimate tiles, mortars, and membranes in your basement for 48 to 72 hours so shifts in subterranean temperature do not disrupt bonding.\n- Assembly compatibility: Verify that their thin-set mortar, grout, and membrane systems are fully compatible with below-grade concrete.\n\nIf your slab shows active water intrusion or significant slope issues, professional remediation must happen first. Abraham Becerra and the team at HH Remodeling LLC handle basement tile installations with hands-on care across the Denver area. Reach out at 303-505-0866 to discuss your project.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
